<li>gpt-oss-20b: 55.84 → 106.64 tok/s (1.91×) decode, 5 repetitions</li>
<li>gpt-oss-120b on two cards: 33.27 → 67.14 tok/s (2.02×) decode, 2 repetitions</li>
<li>gpt-oss-120b prompt processing: 594.5 → 600.1 tok/s</li>
<li>gpt-oss-20b (8 × 2048 tokens) perplexity: 14.5148 → 14.5129</li>
</ul>
<p dir="auto"><strong>Changes:</strong></p>
<ul dir="auto">
<li>Replaces table-based MXFP4 decoding with bit-identical 32-bit arithmetic, avoiding costly permute emulation.</li>
<li>Adds lazy, per-expert weight reordering to enable aligned vector loads, with reorder-aware decode, multi-token, and prefill paths.</li>
<li>Limits temporary reorder memory to 32 MiB and preserves the generic fallback for misaligned tensors.</li>
</ul>
<h2 dir="auto">Additional information</h2>
<p dir="auto">This was tested and benchmarked on Intel Arc Pro B70 GPUs running Windows 11 and oneAPI 2026.0 and AMD EPYC 7402P, with flash attention enabled.  Gpt-oss-20b ran on one card and gpt-oss-120b on two cards.</p>
<p dir="auto">All MXFP4 backend tests pass on both devices: MUL_MAT 39/39 and MUL_MAT_ID 74/74. gpt-oss-20b perplexity remains within noise of the baseline.</p>
<p dir="auto">Arithmetic decoding is the prerequisite for the weight-reordering optimization. Other GPUs and Linux have not been tested.</p>
<p dir="auto">Prior to this change, the SYCL backend never created reorder metadata for the MXFP4 tensors, so the existing MXFP4 MUL_MAT_ID tests never exercised a reordered kernel.</p>
<h2 dir="auto">Requirements</h2>

<ul dir="auto">
<li>
<p dir="auto">I have read and agree with the <a href="https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/blob/master/CONTRIBUTING.md">contributing guidelines</a> - YES</p>
</li>
<li>
<p dir="auto">AI usage disclosure: YES - Opus 5.5 has been helping me hill-climb efficiencies with the Intel Arc platform. This seems like one the rest of the community would benefit from.</p>
</li>
</ul>

